1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,200
At the end of September 2026, a Boeing 737
took off from Dubai, heading for Tel Aviv.

2
00:00:07,861 --> 00:00:10,201
Somewhere along the way,
the co-pilot attacked

3
00:00:10,431 --> 00:00:13,121
the captain with the
cockpit's emergency axe.

4
00:00:13,803 --> 00:00:17,343
The airliner dropped more than
four kilometres in about a minute.

5
00:00:17,973 --> 00:00:22,293
The injured captain, lying on the floor,
managed to unlock the cockpit door.

6
00:00:22,933 --> 00:00:25,593
Passengers rushed in and
overpowered the co-pilot.

7
00:00:26,575 --> 00:00:28,215
And there was one more thing.

8
00:00:28,815 --> 00:00:31,835
Two spare flydubai pilots
were sitting in the cabin.

9
00:00:32,319 --> 00:00:36,379
They took the controls and
landed in Tabuk, in Saudi Arabia.

10
00:00:37,001 --> 00:00:40,581
They were only there because the
airline expected a long detour.

11
00:00:41,141 --> 00:00:45,521
So here's the uncomfortable
question. What if they hadn't been?

12
00:00:45,947 --> 00:00:47,827
What if everyone behind
that door was just...

13
00:00:48,057 --> 00:00:49,547
a passenger?

14
00:00:50,167 --> 00:00:51,887
How would they get the plane down?

15
00:00:54,109 --> 00:00:58,389
In a 2023 survey, almost half
of American men said they could

16
00:00:58,619 --> 00:01:02,769
land a passenger plane, with
only air traffic control to help.

17
00:01:03,378 --> 00:01:06,578
One airline pilot put their
chances at almost zero.

18
00:01:07,298 --> 00:01:08,838
In a way, both are right.

19
00:01:09,513 --> 00:01:13,073
An untrained passenger almost
certainly can't fly an airliner.

20
00:01:13,273 --> 00:01:15,653
But they might not have to.

21
00:01:15,861 --> 00:01:18,581
Because a modern airliner
can already land itself.

22
00:01:18,661 --> 00:01:21,821
Not in the future. Today.

23
00:01:22,353 --> 00:01:25,533
So the passengers don't need
to learn how to fly the plane.

24
00:01:26,073 --> 00:01:29,153
They need to work out how to
make the plane fly itself.

25
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:32,780
That sounds simple. It isn't.

26
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:35,780
And the first problem has
nothing to do with flying.

27
00:01:37,087 --> 00:01:38,287
It's a door.

28
00:01:39,481 --> 00:01:42,261
Since the attacks of 2001, cockpit doors

29
00:01:42,491 --> 00:01:45,101
have been reinforced
and locked from inside.

30
00:01:45,721 --> 00:01:47,741
They're built to keep people out.

31
00:01:48,069 --> 00:01:50,609
But the cabin crew know an emergency code.

32
00:01:51,329 --> 00:01:53,929
If nobody in the cockpit
denies access within a set

33
00:01:54,159 --> 00:01:57,289
time, often under half a
minute, the door unlocks.

34
00:01:59,819 --> 00:02:03,339
And once the passengers are
inside, there's some good news.

35
00:02:03,675 --> 00:02:08,755
At cruising altitude, the plane is almost
certainly flying on autopilot already.

36
00:02:09,403 --> 00:02:14,443
It holds its height and speed, follows its
route, without anyone touching anything.

37
00:02:14,760 --> 00:02:18,180
So the most important rule is
the one that sounds the laziest.

38
00:02:18,260 --> 00:02:20,280
Don't touch anything.

39
00:02:20,981 --> 00:02:23,481
The autopilot won't make any decisions.

40
00:02:23,561 --> 00:02:26,061
But it will keep the plane flying.

41
00:02:26,332 --> 00:02:31,012
And that buys them the most valuable
thing in this whole story. Time.

42
00:02:33,322 --> 00:02:35,042
But time doesn't last forever.

43
00:02:36,042 --> 00:02:40,322
In 2005, a Helios Airways
737 climbed without its

44
00:02:40,552 --> 00:02:43,842
cabin pressurised, and
both pilots passed out.

45
00:02:44,449 --> 00:02:46,809
The autopilot flew on to Athens, and

46
00:02:47,039 --> 00:02:49,529
circled there, perfectly,
for over an hour.

47
00:02:50,028 --> 00:02:53,868
A flight attendant with a pilot's
licence finally got into the cockpit.

48
00:02:54,728 --> 00:02:57,268
But by then, the fuel was running out.

49
00:02:57,908 --> 00:03:00,128
A hundred and twenty-one people died.

50
00:03:01,395 --> 00:03:05,515
So the autopilot gives you time.
It doesn't give you a plan.

51
00:03:08,005 --> 00:03:11,865
For that, the passengers need someone
who actually knows this aircraft.

52
00:03:12,625 --> 00:03:14,065
And that means the radio.

53
00:03:14,887 --> 00:03:19,967
Not a phone. Eleven kilometres up,
a phone often has no signal at all.

54
00:03:20,510 --> 00:03:22,450
The radio is probably still set to the

55
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:25,310
last controller the
pilots were talking to.

56
00:03:25,610 --> 00:03:29,210
And the button to talk is
right on the control column.

57
00:03:29,803 --> 00:03:32,443
Press it, and say:
"Mayday, mayday, mayday.

58
00:03:32,523 --> 00:03:35,143
The pilots are incapacitated."

59
00:03:36,367 --> 00:03:41,427
That works. In 2022, a passenger
called Darren Harrison did

60
00:03:41,657 --> 00:03:44,767
exactly this, in a small
Cessna over Florida.

61
00:03:45,489 --> 00:03:47,069
His pilot had collapsed.

62
00:03:47,709 --> 00:03:49,649
Harrison had never flown a plane.

63
00:03:50,129 --> 00:03:54,249
"I have no idea how to fly the
airplane," he told the controller.

64
00:03:55,019 --> 00:03:57,219
Controllers found Robert Morgan, a

65
00:03:57,449 --> 00:03:59,719
controller who was also
a flight instructor.

66
00:04:00,112 --> 00:04:04,112
And Morgan talked him down, step by
step, onto the runway at Palm Beach.

67
00:04:06,572 --> 00:04:08,492
But a Cessna isn't a 737.

68
00:04:09,492 --> 00:04:14,492
An airliner lands about twice as fast,
and can weigh nearly twenty times as much.

69
00:04:15,203 --> 00:04:18,223
Flying that onto a runway
by hand, with no experience,

70
00:04:18,453 --> 00:04:21,543
is where the optimism
of that survey runs out.

71
00:04:24,043 --> 00:04:27,443
So the voice on the radio isn't
going to teach them to fly.

72
00:04:28,043 --> 00:04:31,083
It's going to help them use
the plane's own automation.

73
00:04:31,649 --> 00:04:34,529
And this is where having
several passengers helps.

74
00:04:35,289 --> 00:04:37,169
One sits in the seat and talks.

75
00:04:37,889 --> 00:04:39,929
One reads out what the screens say.

76
00:04:40,239 --> 00:04:43,839
And if the plane has wi-fi, a
third might even get a video call

77
00:04:44,069 --> 00:04:47,499
through to the airline, and point
the camera at the instruments.

78
00:04:48,071 --> 00:04:50,711
Nobody has to understand
the whole aircraft.

79
00:04:51,191 --> 00:04:54,991
They just have to describe
it, one instrument at a time.

80
00:04:55,550 --> 00:04:57,370
On the ground, the controller can bring in

81
00:04:57,600 --> 00:05:00,470
an airline pilot who
flies this exact type.

82
00:05:02,970 --> 00:05:06,150
Then comes the part most
passengers don't know exists.

83
00:05:06,770 --> 00:05:09,970
Many large airports have an
instrument landing system.

84
00:05:10,621 --> 00:05:12,921
It tells a plane two
things: where the runway

85
00:05:13,151 --> 00:05:15,981
is, and at what angle
to descend towards it.

86
00:05:16,098 --> 00:05:17,878
And an autopilot that's equipped for it

87
00:05:18,108 --> 00:05:20,958
can follow those signals,
all the way down.

88
00:05:21,227 --> 00:05:23,867
On many airliners, it doesn't stop there.

89
00:05:24,059 --> 00:05:27,879
Set up correctly, it can raise
the nose just before touchdown,

90
00:05:28,279 --> 00:05:31,379
pull the engines back to idle,
and put the wheels on the runway.

91
00:05:32,177 --> 00:05:34,217
Pilots call it autoland.

92
00:05:34,497 --> 00:05:38,437
It was built for fog, for landing when
the crew can barely see the runway.

93
00:05:39,229 --> 00:05:44,389
The first airliner to land itself with
passengers on board did it in 1965.

94
00:05:45,427 --> 00:05:48,707
So can an airliner land itself? Yes.

95
00:05:48,937 --> 00:05:51,407
It's been doing it for over sixty years.

96
00:05:54,487 --> 00:05:56,207
But it can't decide to.

97
00:05:57,397 --> 00:05:59,617
Somebody still has to tell it to.

98
00:06:02,007 --> 00:06:03,587
And that's the best case.

99
00:06:04,247 --> 00:06:06,167
The runway needs the right equipment.

100
00:06:06,727 --> 00:06:08,587
The plane needs the right systems.

101
00:06:09,024 --> 00:06:13,404
And if anything goes wrong, nobody in
that cockpit knows what they're doing.

102
00:06:14,384 --> 00:06:17,284
Worse, the plane may not be
able to land itself at all.

103
00:06:17,844 --> 00:06:22,404
The runway might have no landing system,
or something on board might have failed.

104
00:06:22,684 --> 00:06:25,324
Then someone has to fly it down by hand.

105
00:06:25,781 --> 00:06:28,921
The voice on the radio
can explain every control.

106
00:06:29,001 --> 00:06:31,701
But it can't move a single one of them.

107
00:06:32,436 --> 00:06:34,316
So how would that actually go?

108
00:06:35,036 --> 00:06:38,136
In 2023, the Washington Post tried it.

109
00:06:38,816 --> 00:06:43,516
Four people who had never flown, an Airbus
simulator, and instructors on the radio.

110
00:06:44,005 --> 00:06:46,885
Two of them switched off
the autopilot, and crashed.

111
00:06:47,664 --> 00:06:50,204
One touched down, and ran off the runway.

112
00:06:50,644 --> 00:06:54,564
One refused to land on the runway
at all, and headed for a lake.

113
00:06:55,186 --> 00:06:57,086
The only good landing
came after the instructors

114
00:06:57,316 --> 00:07:01,126
stopped the simulation and
coached her in, step by step.

115
00:07:03,726 --> 00:07:06,106
Which leads to a choice that feels wrong.

116
00:07:06,806 --> 00:07:10,346
If the landing might go badly,
why not aim for something soft?

117
00:07:10,992 --> 00:07:13,072
A field. A desert. The sea.

118
00:07:13,997 --> 00:07:16,757
Because a runway isn't
hard. It's forgiving.

119
00:07:18,128 --> 00:07:21,388
It's kilometres long, flat,
and clear of obstacles.

120
00:07:21,948 --> 00:07:24,348
Fire crews can be waiting beside it.

121
00:07:24,428 --> 00:07:28,028
And if you land too fast,
there's room left to stop.

122
00:07:28,617 --> 00:07:31,797
A field has ditches, fences and trees.

123
00:07:32,417 --> 00:07:36,217
And water can rip an airliner apart
if the plane hits at the wrong angle.

124
00:07:36,415 --> 00:07:40,615
Even the famous landing on the
Hudson in 2009 was the work

125
00:07:40,845 --> 00:07:43,955
of a captain with almost twenty
thousand hours of flying.

126
00:07:44,661 --> 00:07:49,521
So if a suitable airport is within reach,
the answer is almost always the runway.

127
00:07:50,482 --> 00:07:53,962
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

128
00:07:54,582 --> 00:07:56,182
It really helps the channel.

129
00:07:58,572 --> 00:08:00,592
Which leaves one last question.

130
00:08:01,272 --> 00:08:05,512
If an airliner can land itself, why can't
someone just take over from the ground?

131
00:08:05,943 --> 00:08:08,783
Because a link that lets someone
fly an airliner from the ground

132
00:08:09,013 --> 00:08:13,663
would be a security problem almost
as big as the emergency itself.

133
00:08:15,023 --> 00:08:17,903
So aviation is looking
in a different direction.

134
00:08:18,623 --> 00:08:20,163
Not a pilot on the ground.

135
00:08:20,783 --> 00:08:22,623
A plane that doesn't need one.

136
00:08:23,187 --> 00:08:28,167
Since 2020, some small private planes have
had a system called Emergency Autoland.

137
00:08:29,641 --> 00:08:31,621
One button, meant for passengers.

138
00:08:32,561 --> 00:08:34,521
The plane picks an
airport, tells air traffic

139
00:08:34,751 --> 00:08:37,021
control what's happening, flies there.

140
00:08:37,601 --> 00:08:39,741
Then it lands, and brakes to a stop.

141
00:08:40,365 --> 00:08:44,705
In December 2025, it was used
for real for the first time.

142
00:08:45,525 --> 00:08:48,125
A King Air lost cabin
pressure over Colorado.

143
00:08:49,081 --> 00:08:52,761
Both pilots were on oxygen and
chose to let the system fly.

144
00:08:53,081 --> 00:08:56,221
It landed near Denver,
and everyone walked away.

145
00:08:58,551 --> 00:09:02,411
Nothing like that is fitted to
the airliners most of us fly on.

146
00:09:03,071 --> 00:09:05,931
But Airbus has tested the idea on an A350.

147
00:09:06,692 --> 00:09:09,612
In flight tests, the plane
responded to simulated pilot

148
00:09:09,842 --> 00:09:14,272
incapacitation, planned a new route,
and talked to air traffic control.

149
00:09:17,062 --> 00:09:19,442
So, could passengers land an airliner?

150
00:09:20,715 --> 00:09:22,715
Probably not by flying it.

151
00:09:22,875 --> 00:09:24,695
But they might not have to.

152
00:09:24,951 --> 00:09:28,331
If the plane is stable, the
autopilot is working, and they

153
00:09:28,561 --> 00:09:32,131
can reach someone on the ground,
their job changes completely.

154
00:09:32,897 --> 00:09:34,837
They don't have to become pilots.

155
00:09:35,517 --> 00:09:38,797
They have to become the hands and
eyes of the people on the ground.

156
00:09:39,223 --> 00:09:40,843
And the plane can do the flying.

157
00:09:42,543 --> 00:09:45,903
The safest airliner isn't
one that never needs a pilot.

158
00:09:46,414 --> 00:09:48,924
It's one that knows what to
do when the pilot is gone.
